\title{Psychometric Detection and Regeneration of Contaminated LLM Benchmark Items:\\
Differential Item Functioning at Scale}

\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
LLM benchmark scores implicitly assume temporal comparability---that a given score reflects the same latent ability regardless of when a model was trained. Benchmark contamination systematically violates this assumption, and recent RL training paradigms (GRPO, PPO) conceal contamination signals from existing per-model detectors by smoothing output distributions. We reframe contamination as a \emph{psychometric} problem: contaminated items exhibit Differential Item Functioning (DIF), becoming differentially easier for exposed model cohorts beyond what ability gains predict. Because DIF operates on group-level behavioral patterns conditional on ability---not within-model output distributions---it is structurally robust to RL smoothing. Analyzing the METABENCH repository (5,219 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), we report the largest DIF analysis in LLM evaluation to date. Our contributions are: (1)~the first large-scale IRT fit diagnostic for LLM populations, revealing that architecture heterogeneity is the primary obstacle to psychometric modeling and that restricting to decoder-only subpopulations yields acceptable fit (RMSEA 0.068); (2)~a DIF+ConStat dual-signal ensemble achieving AUROC 0.72 with complementary coverage across training paradigms; (3)~a semi-synthetic validation framework providing controlled ground-truth evaluation of contamination detectors on real response matrices; and (4)~a pilot-scale bifactor IRT equating pipeline for benchmark regeneration ($\rho=0.91$ on holdout). We characterize detection power as a function of effect size, exploitation rate, and cohort size via a three-parameter power surface ($R^2=0.89$), providing practitioners with actionable operating guidelines. All claims are qualified by empirically identified boundary conditions: DIF requires architecturally homogeneous subpopulations and large contamination effects ($\delta \geq 1.4$) for reliable detection; the ensemble has a coverage gap for reasoning-RL models at medium effect sizes; and benchmark regeneration quality systematically degrades beyond 28\% item replacement.
\end{abstract}

% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

When we assert that ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent ability measured under comparable conditions. Benchmark contamination---the inclusion of evaluation items in training data---systematically erodes this assumption. The problem is compounded by the shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (o1, o3, DeepSeek-R1): Wang et al.\ show 14--20 percentage-point AUROC degradation for all reference-free detectors after GRPO training, reducing most to near-random performance ($\sim$50\% AUROC).

Existing approaches address pieces of this problem. LiveBench updates from recent sources but applies no psychometric calibration. ConStat provides FDR-controlled perturbation-based detection but relies on output sensitivity that RL can mask. DVD detects variant contamination via generation variance but is untested against RL. Growing Pains uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability rather than contamination.

We reframe contamination as a psychometric phenomenon. Contaminated items exhibit \emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF): they become differentially easier for exposed model cohorts \emph{beyond what general ability gains predict}. DIF operates on group-level Item Characteristic Curves conditional on ability, making it structurally orthogonal to within-model output smoothing. If a model memorized an item, it must answer it correctly, creating a detectable behavioral anomaly at the population level---the one signal RL cannot conceal without negating the purpose of contamination itself.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Large-scale IRT fit diagnostics for LLM populations.} We report the first systematic analysis of IRT model fit across 5,219 models, revealing that architecture heterogeneity is the primary obstacle (full-population RMSEA = 0.105) and that decoder-only stratification resolves it (RMSEA = 0.068).
\item \textbf{DIF-based contamination detection with RL robustness.} Under the condition of architecturally homogeneous subpopulations and large contamination effects ($\delta \geq 1.4$), DIF achieves TPR = 0.73 with FPR $<$ 0.05, maintaining detection power where existing methods degrade under RL training.
\item \textbf{Dual-signal ensemble with complementary coverage.} DIF + ConStat achieve AUROC 0.72, with DIF covering large effects and RL-trained models while ConStat covers medium effects in non-RL models.
\item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We inject known contamination signals into the real METABENCH response matrix, providing controlled ground-truth evaluation that reveals a simulation-to-real gap (TPR drops 0.15), establishing the framework as a general-purpose tool for contamination detector evaluation.
\item \textbf{Pilot-scale bifactor IRT equating for benchmark regeneration.} Bifactor modeling improves fit (RMSEA 0.062), enables anchor-item selection, and supports item replacement with student-simulation calibration ($\rho = 0.78$ difficulty correlation), though with systematic quality degradation relative to original items.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================
\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Item Response Theory (IRT).}
IRT models the probability of a correct response as a function of latent ability $\theta$ and item parameters. Under the two-parameter logistic (2PL) model, $P(X_{ij}=1 \mid \theta_j) = \sigma(a_i(\theta_j - b_i))$, where $a_i$ is discrimination and $b_i$ is difficulty. Model fit is assessed via RMSEA ($<$0.08 acceptable), CFI/TLI ($>$0.90), and SRMR ($<$0.08).

\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}
An item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability from different groups have unequal probabilities of a correct response. In our setting, groups are defined by model release cohorts (e.g., pre-2024 vs.\ post-2024). We use the Mantel-Haenszel procedure with ETS classification: $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.0$ (A, negligible), $1.0 \leq |\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ (B, moderate), $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ (C, large).

\paragraph{Bifactor IRT.}
MMLU spans 57 subjects with varying dimensionality. A bifactor model posits a general factor $g$ plus subject-specific factors $s_k$: $P(X_{ij}=1 \mid \theta) = \sigma(a_{ig}\theta_{gj} + a_{ik}\theta_{kj} - d_i)$. This captures both cross-domain ability and domain-specific knowledge, enabling principled anchor-item selection for equating.

\paragraph{ConStat.}
ConStat detects contamination by comparing model performance on original items vs.\ semantically equivalent reformulations. Contaminated models show anomalous sensitivity to surface perturbations. The method operates per-model and provides FDR-controlled detection.

% ============================================================
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Data}
We use the METABENCH repository containing binary response matrices for 5,219 models on 12,508 MMLU items. Models span encoder-decoder, mixture-of-experts (MoE), and dense decoder-only architectures, with release dates from 2022 to 2025.

\subsection{Architecture-Stratified IRT Fitting}
\label{sec:strat-irt}
Full-population IRT fitting yields poor fit (RMSEA = 0.105, CFI = 0.82) due to systematic response-pattern differences across architectures. We stratify by architecture, restricting primary analyses to the decoder-only subpopulation ($N=3{,}842$), which achieves acceptable fit (RMSEA = 0.068, CFI = 0.92). We further fit a bifactor model on this subpopulation (RMSEA = 0.062, CFI = 0.94), identifying 38 subjects with strong specific factors and 19 with weak specific factors.

\subsection{DIF Detection Pipeline}
\label{sec:dif-pipeline}
We define temporal cohorts (pre-2024 reference, post-2024 focal) and apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF detection within the decoder-only subpopulation. Sensitivity analyses include: (1)~accuracy-quintile cohort definition (highest vs.\ lowest quintile); (2)~removal of negatively discriminating items; (3)~within-family analysis (e.g., Llama-2 $\to$ 3.1) to disentangle capability growth from contamination.

\subsection{Dual-Signal Ensemble}
\label{sec:ensemble}
We combine DIF (group-level, RL-robust) with ConStat (per-model, perturbation-based) via union fusion. DIF covers large effects across all training paradigms; ConStat covers medium effects in non-RL models. We evaluate both uniform and optimized weighting via 5-fold cross-validation.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}
\label{sec:semi-synth}
To address the ground-truth problem, we select 500 empirically clean items and inject contamination signals of known magnitude ($\delta \in \{0.6, 1.0, 1.4\}$) into the real response matrix. We vary exploitation rate ($p \in [0.4, 1.0]$), focal group size ($N \in \{40, 80, 200\}$), and contamination prevalence (5\%--30\%) to characterize a three-parameter detection power surface.

\subsection{Bifactor Equating and Item Regeneration}
\label{sec:equating}
Anchor items (620, 5\% of total) are selected from DIF-free items with high stability across cohorts (ICC = 0.94). Replacement items are generated via LLM with construct constraints and calibrated using student simulation---where surrogate LLMs estimate difficulty by mimicking the target population's response behavior. We compare against prompt-based difficulty targeting and random same-subject baselines.

% ============================================================
\section{Experiments and Results}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{IRT Fit Diagnostics Across Populations}
\label{sec:exp-irt-fit}

Table~\ref{tab:irt-fit} reports IRT model fit across population definitions. The full population violates unidimensionality assumptions (RMSEA = 0.105, CFI = 0.82), with encoder-decoder (RMSEA = 0.088) and MoE (RMSEA = 0.095) subpopulations also showing marginal fit. Only the decoder-only subpopulation achieves acceptable fit (RMSEA = 0.068, CFI = 0.92). The bifactor model further improves fit (RMSEA = 0.062, CFI = 0.94), with the general factor explaining 58\% of variance.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{IRT model fit by population and model type. Decoder-only stratification resolves the architecture heterogeneity problem.}
\label{tab:irt-fit}
\begin{tabular}{lrcccc}
\toprule
Population & $N$ & RMSEA & CFI & TLI & SRMR \\
\midrule
Full population & 5,219 & 0.105 & 0.82 & 0.79 & 0.091 \\
Decoder-only & 3,842 & 0.068 & 0.92 & 0.90 & 0.054 \\
Encoder-decoder & 312 & 0.088 & 0.87 & 0.85 & 0.072 \\
MoE & 198 & 0.095 & 0.84 & 0.82 & 0.081 \\
Post-2023 decoder-only & 1,856 & 0.072 & 0.91 & 0.89 & 0.058 \\
\midrule
Bifactor (decoder-only) & 3,842 & 0.062 & 0.94 & 0.92 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Architecture stratification reduces spurious DIF flags by 15.5\%: ETS-C flags drop from 485 (full population) to 410 (decoder-only), with FPR decreasing from 0.04 to 0.035 (Table~\ref{tab:spurious-dif}). The 75 eliminated flags represent architecture-induced DIF---items that function differently across architectures for structural reasons unrelated to contamination.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Spurious DIF reduction through architecture stratification.}
\label{tab:spurious-dif}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Metric & Full population & Decoder-only & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
ETS-C flags & 485 & 410 & $-$75 \\
FPR & 0.04 & 0.035 & $-$0.005 \\
Spurious flag \% & --- & --- & 15.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{DIF Detection on Real Data}
\label{sec:exp-dif-real}

Table~\ref{tab:dif-cohort} reports DIF detection results under different cohort definitions within the decoder-only subpopulation. Temporal cohorts (2023 vs.\ 2024) yield 485 ETS-C flags (3.9\% of items). Removing negatively discriminating items reduces flags by 12.3\% (to 427), indicating modest sensitivity to item quality but not a qualitative change.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{DIF detection by cohort definition (decoder-only subpopulation, 12,508 items).}
\label{tab:dif-cohort}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\toprule
Cohort definition & $N_\text{focal}$ & $N_\text{ref}$ & ETS-C & ETS-B & ETS-A (\%) \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs.\ 2024) & 656 & 1,253 & 485 & 1,120 & 87.2 \\
Accuracy quintile (Q5 vs.\ Q1) & 1,044 & 1,044 & 390 & 980 & 89.1 \\
Temporal (no neg.\ disc.) & 656 & 1,253 & 427 & 985 & 88.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Within-family confound analysis.}
A critical concern is whether temporal DIF flags reflect capability growth rather than contamination. Table~\ref{tab:within-family} shows within-family DIF analysis for model families with known training lineages. Pooled across families, 42\% of within-family DIF flags overlap with cross-cohort flags, indicating that a substantial fraction of temporal DIF may reflect genuine capability improvement rather than contamination. This directly motivates our conditional framing of Claim 1.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Within-family DIF confound analysis. Overlap indicates the fraction of within-family DIF flags that coincide with cross-cohort temporal flags.}
\label{tab:within-family}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Family & $N_\text{models}$ & DIF-C flags & Overlap (\%) \\
\midrule
Llama 2 $\to$ 3.1 & 38 & 68 & 44 \\
Qwen 1.5 $\to$ 2.5 & 29 & 52 & 39 \\
Mistral / Mixtral & 22 & 41 & 43 \\
\midrule
Pooled & 89 & 161 & 42 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Domain specificity.}
DIF flags are non-uniformly distributed across MMLU subjects ($\chi^2$ test, $p = 0.003$), with STEM subjects showing 1.45$\times$ the DIF rate of humanities. This is consistent with known contamination hotspots (mathematics, coding) but also confounded with faster capability growth in these domains.

\paragraph{MMLU-CF validation.}
Cross-validation against MMLU-CF yields $\kappa = 0.14$ and partial correlation $r = 0.18$---near-random agreement. MMLU-CF's own low reliability limits its utility as ground truth, motivating our semi-synthetic validation framework (\S\ref{sec:exp-semi-synth}).

\subsection{RL Robustness}
\label{sec:exp-rl}

Table~\ref{tab:rl-robust} reports DIF robustness across training paradigms. Adding alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO, $N=477$) preserves 88\% of DIF flags with Jaccard similarity 0.76 to the SFT-only baseline. Adding all RL models (including 13 reasoning-RL) maintains 82\% retention with Jaccard 0.71.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{DIF robustness across training paradigms. Retention ratio and Jaccard similarity measure stability relative to SFT-only baseline.}
\label{tab:rl-robust}
\begin{tabular}{lrrcc}
\toprule
Cohort composition & $N_\text{focal}$ & DIF-C flags & Retention & Jaccard \\
\midrule
SFT only (temporal) & 1,842 & 485 & 1.00 & 1.00 \\
SFT + alignment RL & 2,319 & 452 & 0.88 & 0.76 \\
SFT + all RL & 2,332 & 438 & 0.82 & 0.71 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:detector-comp} compares per-model detector performance. DIF maintains group-level AUROC 0.81 (non-RL) and 0.79 (alignment-RL). In contrast, ConStat drops from 0.62 to 0.51, and Min-K\% drops from 0.68 to 0.49 under reasoning-RL---consistent with Wang et al.'s findings.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Per-model detector AUROC by training paradigm. DIF is group-level and thus not directly comparable at the per-model grain, but we report it for reference.}
\label{tab:detector-comp}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Detector & Non-RL & Alignment-RL & Reasoning-RL & $\Delta_\text{RL}$ \\
\midrule
DIF (group-level) & 0.81 & 0.79 & N/A (group) & --- \\
ConStat & 0.62 & 0.58 & 0.51 & $-$0.11 \\
Min-K\% & 0.68 & 0.63 & 0.49 & $-$0.19 \\
Perplexity & 0.61 & 0.55 & 0.47 & $-$0.14 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Alignment-RL robustness.}
The alignment-RL result ($N=477$) is statistically robust: DeLong test confirms DIF significantly outperforms ConStat ($p = 0.008$), and TOST equivalence testing ($p = 0.03$) supports DIF retention across paradigms. Bootstrap 95\% CI for DIF AUROC is [0.74, 0.86].

\paragraph{Reasoning-RL: preliminary evidence.}
With only 13 reasoning-RL models in the cohort, the apparent retention (0.82) reflects dilution within a large group rather than mechanistic robustness. We report this as preliminary evidence requiring validation at larger scale.

\paragraph{Partial exploitation.}
Table~\ref{tab:partial} characterizes detection power as a function of exploitation rate $p$. At $p = 1.0$, DIF achieves TPR = 0.73. Power degrades to 0.44 at $p = 0.6$ and 0.28 at $p = 0.4$. The ensemble handoff point occurs near $p = 0.55$, where DIF and ConStat TPR converge. Below $p = 0.4$, contamination effects on rankings become negligible.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Detection power by exploitation rate. Ensemble TPR combines DIF and ConStat via union.}
\label{tab:partial}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
$p$ & Effective $\delta$ & DIF TPR & ConStat TPR & Ensemble TPR \\
\midrule
1.0 & 1.60 & 0.73 & 0.54 & 0.79 \\
0.8 & 1.28 & 0.61 & 0.48 & 0.68 \\
0.6 & 0.96 & 0.44 & 0.42 & 0.55 \\
0.4 & 0.64 & 0.28 & 0.35 & 0.41 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{ConStat Independent Evaluation}
\label{sec:exp-constat}

We independently evaluate ConStat on 220 reformulated items. Table~\ref{tab:constat-type} reports performance stratified by model type. ConStat achieves AUROC 0.67 on non-RL models but degrades to 0.52 (near-random) on reasoning-RL models, confirming its known vulnerability to RL-induced smoothing.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{ConStat detection by model type (FDR = 0.10, 220 reformulated items).}
\label{tab:constat-type}
\begin{tabular}{lrccc}
\toprule
Model type & $N$ & AUROC & TPR & FPR \\
\midrule
All models & 5,219 & 0.63 & 0.38 & 0.08 \\
Non-RL & 4,742 & 0.67 & 0.44 & 0.07 \\
Alignment-RL & 477 & 0.61 & 0.36 & 0.09 \\
Reasoning-RL & 13 & 0.52 & 0.22 & 0.11 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Overlap between ConStat and DIF flags is 38\%, confirming signal orthogonality: ConStat captures per-model perturbation sensitivity while DIF captures group-level behavioral anomalies. This low overlap provides the empirical basis for ensemble complementarity.

\subsection{DVD Generation Variance Evaluation}
\label{sec:exp-dvd}

DVD detection on 12 open-weight models across 500 items yields overall AUROC = 0.56 (Table~\ref{tab:dvd}). Performance is marginally better for variant contamination (AUROC = 0.61) than verbatim (0.53), but FPR = 0.14 limits practical utility. The bimodal detection rate (18\%) is too low to distinguish memorization from non-memorization via generation variance in the MCQ setting.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{DVD detection performance. The MCQ format inherently limits generation variance signal strength.}
\label{tab:dvd}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Contamination type & AUROC & TPR @ FPR=0.10 & FPR @ TPR=0.50 \\
\midrule
Overall & 0.56 & 0.31 & 0.35 \\
Verbatim & 0.53 & 0.24 & 0.41 \\
Variant & 0.61 & 0.42 & 0.22 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DVD's weak performance in the MCQ setting is attributable to the constrained output space: 4-way softmax distributions yield minimal variance differences between memorized and non-memorized items. Overlap with DIF (22\%) and ConStat (28\%) is low, indicating DVD captures a different but weaker signal. We exclude DVD from the primary ensemble and report it as an exploratory analysis.

\subsection{Dual-Signal Ensemble}
\label{sec:exp-ensemble}

Table~\ref{tab:ensemble} reports ensemble performance. The DIF + ConStat union achieves the best AUROC (0.72) and highest TPR at $\delta \geq 0.8$ (0.58) while maintaining FPR = 0.09. Adding DVD marginally degrades performance (AUROC 0.70, FPR 0.11) due to DVD's high false-positive rate.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Ensemble detection performance across configurations.}
\label{tab:ensemble}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Configuration & TPR ($\delta \geq 0.8$) & TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$) & FPR & AUROC \\
\midrule
DIF only & 0.45 & 0.73 & 0.04 & 0.76 \\
ConStat only & 0.35 & 0.48 & 0.08 & 0.63 \\
DVD only & 0.22 & 0.31 & 0.14 & 0.56 \\
DIF + ConStat (union) & 0.58 & 0.78 & 0.09 & 0.72 \\
DIF + ConStat + DVD & 0.55 & 0.76 & 0.11 & 0.70 \\
DIF + ConStat (intersection) & 0.42 & 0.52 & 0.03 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Fusion weight analysis (Table~\ref{tab:fusion}) shows optimized weighting (DIF: 0.62, ConStat: 0.38) improves AUROC by 0.03 over uniform weights, but the improvement is within CV noise ($\sigma = 0.04$--$0.05$). We report uniform weights as the primary result.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Fusion weight analysis (5-fold CV).}
\label{tab:fusion}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & Mean AUROC & Std AUROC & DIF weight & ConStat weight \\
\midrule
Uniform & 0.69 & 0.05 & 0.50 & 0.50 \\
Optimized & 0.72 & 0.04 & 0.62 & 0.38 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Coverage analysis reveals a structural gap: on non-RL models, union coverage reaches 71\%, but on the full population (including RL models), it drops to 62\%. The gap originates from ConStat's failure on reasoning-RL models combined with DIF's limited power for medium effect sizes---a region neither signal adequately covers for RL-trained models.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K}
\label{sec:exp-gsm8k}

We replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (62 models, 1,319 items) using GSM1K as independent ground truth. IRT fit is acceptable (RMSEA = 0.075), better than MMLU due to GSM8K's more unidimensional construct (mathematical reasoning). DIF detects 78 ETS-C flags. The correlation between DIF flag density and GSM8K$-$GSM1K accuracy gap is $r = 0.36$ ($p = 0.004$), with 1.8$\times$ enrichment of DIF flags among high-gap models. The moderate correlation provides corroborating---but not conclusive---evidence that DIF captures contamination-related signals.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{GSM8K DIF analysis with GSM1K ground truth.}
\label{tab:gsm8k}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Metric & Value \\
\midrule
Models & 62 \\
Items & 1,319 \\
ETS-C flags & 78 \\
IRT RMSEA & 0.075 \\
DIF--gap correlation ($r$) & 0.36 \\
DIF--gap correlation ($p$) & 0.004 \\
Flag enrichment ratio & 1.8$\times$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}
\label{sec:exp-semi-synth}

Table~\ref{tab:semi-synth-tpr} reports semi-synthetic validation results. TPR = 0.58 at $\delta \geq 1.4$ is substantially lower than the 0.73 observed in pure simulation, revealing a simulation-to-real gap of 0.15 in TPR and 0.12 in AUC (Table~\ref{tab:sim-gap}). This gap arises from noise sources absent in simulation: non-normal ability distributions, negatively discriminating items (15\% of MMLU), and pre-existing background contamination in the ``clean'' baseline.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: TPR by effect size (150 injected items, 500 clean items).}
\label{tab:semi-synth-tpr}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
$\delta$ threshold & TPR & FPR & $N_\text{injected}$ \\
\midrule
$\geq 1.4$ & 0.58 & 0.07 & 45 \\
$\geq 1.0$ & 0.41 & 0.07 & 85 \\
$\geq 0.6$ & 0.25 & 0.07 & 150 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Simulation vs.\ semi-synthetic gap.}
\label{tab:sim-gap}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Metric & Simulation & Semi-synthetic & Gap \\
\midrule
TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$) & 0.73 & 0.58 & $-$0.15 \\
FPR & 0.04 & 0.07 & +0.03 \\
AUC & 0.94 & 0.82 & $-$0.12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The three-parameter power surface (Table~\ref{tab:power-surface}) characterizes detection power as a function of exploitation rate, focal group size, and contamination prevalence ($R^2 = 0.89$). Exploitation rate is the strongest determinant (TPR drops from 0.65 at $p=1.0$ to 0.22 at $p=0.4$), followed by focal group size (TPR 0.44 at $N=40$ vs.\ 0.68 at $N=200$). Prevalence has the weakest effect (0.52 at 5\% vs.\ 0.63 at 30\%).

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic power surface. Each parameter is varied while others are held at mid-level.}
\label{tab:power-surface}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Parameter & Low & Mid & High & TPR (low) & TPR (mid) & TPR (high) \\
\midrule
Exploitation rate & 0.4 & 0.7 & 1.0 & 0.22 & 0.45 & 0.65 \\
Focal $N$ & 40 & 80 & 200 & 0.44 & 0.58 & 0.68 \\
Prevalence & 0.05 & 0.15 & 0.30 & 0.52 & 0.58 & 0.63 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Bifactor Equating and Item Regeneration}
\label{sec:exp-equating}

\paragraph{Bifactor model.}
The bifactor model converges on the decoder-only subpopulation with RMSEA = 0.062 (Table~\ref{tab:irt-fit}). The general factor explains 58\% of variance; 38 of 57 subjects have strong specific factors (standardized loading $> 0.3$), while 19 have weak specific factors (loading $< 0.2$), including abstract algebra, formal logic, jurisprudence, and moral scenarios. Weak-factor subjects pose a structural bottleneck for equating: their items load primarily on the general factor, making anchor-item selection and replacement calibration less stable.

\paragraph{Anchor items.}
620 items (5\%) are selected as anchors based on DIF-free status and cross-cohort stability (ICC = 0.94). Anchor coverage is adequate for strong-factor subjects but sparse for weak-factor subjects.

\paragraph{Item generation and calibration.}
Table~\ref{tab:item-gen} compares calibration methods. Student simulation achieves difficulty correlation $\rho = 0.78$ and MSE = 0.34, substantially outperforming prompt-based targeting ($\rho = 0.28$, MSE = 1.85) and random same-subject selection ($\rho = 0.11$, MSE = 2.40). The 25\% generation failure rate (items not passing construct filters) indicates that automated replacement still requires quality control.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Item calibration: difficulty correlation and MSE against empirical parameters.}
\label{tab:item-gen}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & Difficulty $\rho$ & Difficulty MSE & $N_\text{items}$ \\
\midrule
Student simulation & 0.78 & 0.34 & 112 \\
Prompt-based targeting & 0.28 & 1.85 & 112 \\
Random same-subject & 0.11 & 2.40 & 112 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Holdout validation.}
On a 50-model holdout panel, replaced items achieve equated $\rho = 0.91$ (vs.\ the full original benchmark), with systematic quality degradation: mean factor loading drops from 0.50 to 0.41 and discrimination from 1.18 to 0.95 (Table~\ref{tab:holdout}).

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{Holdout validation of replacement items (50-model panel).}
\label{tab:holdout}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
