% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Methodological contributions.}
Our primary contributions are methodological rather than systemic. First, we provide the first large-scale IRT fit diagnostic for LLM populations, establishing that architecture heterogeneity---not item quality or sample size---is the primary obstacle to psychometric modeling of LLM evaluation data. The practical prescription is straightforward: restrict IRT-based analyses to architecturally homogeneous subpopulations.

Second, the semi-synthetic validation framework provides a general-purpose tool for evaluating any item-level contamination detector under controlled conditions on real response data. The revealed simulation-to-real gap (TPR $-$0.15, AUC $-$0.12) cautions against relying solely on simulation for detector evaluation.

Third, the student-simulation calibration method ($\rho = 0.78$) significantly outperforms prompt-based targeting ($\rho = 0.28$) for replacement item difficulty estimation, establishing a viable calibration strategy for benchmark regeneration.

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
Our claims carry explicit boundary conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item DIF detection requires architecturally homogeneous subpopulations (decoder-only) and is practical only for large effects ($\delta \geq 1.4$). At medium effects ($\delta \in [0.6, 1.4]$), detection power is insufficient without ensemble support.
\item The dual-signal ensemble has a coverage gap: reasoning-RL models at medium effect sizes are inadequately covered by either DIF (insufficient power) or ConStat (RL-degraded).
\item Within-family analysis reveals 42\% overlap between temporal DIF and within-family DIF, meaning nearly half of flagged items may reflect capability growth rather than contamination.
\item Benchmark regeneration quality systematically degrades: replacement items have lower factor loadings (0.41 vs.\ 0.50) and discrimination (0.95 vs.\ 1.18), with a critical replacement threshold at 28\%.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{DVD negative result.}
DVD generation variance detection achieves AUROC = 0.56 in the MCQ setting---marginally above random. The 4-way MCQ output space provides insufficient variance signal, and the bimodal detection rate (18\%) is too low for practical use. We report this as a methodologically informative negative result: generation variance approaches may be better suited to open-ended generation tasks than constrained MCQ formats.

\paragraph{Reasoning-RL coverage gap.}
The most significant limitation is the reasoning-RL validation gap. With only 13 reasoning-RL models available, our DIF retention results (0.82) reflect statistical dilution rather than mechanistic verification. As reasoning-RL models become more prevalent, the decoder-only subpopulation will increasingly contain RL-trained models, potentially changing DIF dynamics. Longitudinal monitoring will be needed to validate DIF stability in this evolving landscape.

% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We present a psychometric approach to benchmark contamination detection and regeneration, demonstrating that DIF analysis provides a structurally RL-robust contamination signal when applied to architecturally homogeneous LLM subpopulations. Our contributions center on detection methodology and psychometric diagnostics rather than a complete end-to-end system: (1)~the first large-scale IRT fit analysis revealing architecture heterogeneity as the primary obstacle; (2)~a dual-signal DIF+ConStat ensemble with complementary coverage (AUROC 0.72); (3)~a semi-synthetic validation framework that quantifies detector performance under controlled conditions; and (4)~pilot-scale bifactor equating for item regeneration ($\rho = 0.91$). All claims are qualified by empirically established boundary conditions---architecture homogeneity, large effect sizes, and limited replacement budgets---and we transparently report both the reasoning-RL coverage gap and the DVD negative result. The three-parameter power surface ($R^2 = 0.89$) provides practitioners with actionable guidelines for deploying DIF-based monitoring in their evaluation pipelines.

