\subsection{Bifactor Equating and Item Regeneration}
\label{sec:exp-equating}

\paragraph{Bifactor model.}
The bifactor model converges on the decoder-only subpopulation with RMSEA = 0.062 (Table~\ref{tab:irt-fit}). The general factor explains 58\% of variance; 38 of 57 subjects have strong specific factors (standardized loading $> 0.3$), while 19 have weak specific factors (loading $< 0.2$), including abstract algebra, formal logic, jurisprudence, and moral scenarios. Weak-factor subjects pose a structural bottleneck for equating: their items load primarily on the general factor, making anchor-item selection and replacement calibration less stable.

\paragraph{Anchor items.}
620 items (5\%) are selected as anchors based on DIF-free status and cross-cohort stability (ICC = 0.94). Anchor coverage is adequate for strong-factor subjects but sparse for weak-factor subjects.

\paragraph{Item generation and calibration.}
Table~\ref{tab:item-gen} compares calibration methods. Student simulation achieves difficulty correlation $\rho = 0.78$ and MSE = 0.34, substantially outperforming prompt-based targeting ($\rho = 0.28$, MSE = 1.85) and random same-subject selection ($\rho = 0.11$, MSE = 2.40). The 25\% generation failure rate (items not passing construct filters) indicates that automated replacement still requires quality control.

